  "image_name": "4cbd8d49df86f0aed898618addb637b8.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure combines a physical picture, a simple model, and the chemical structures of a donor–acceptor system used to study light-driven transport. On the left, the electric field of light is shown rotating as it propagates along the z direction, illustrating two opposite helical polarizations. The field rotates in the transverse plane, so the image emphasizes how circularly polarized light can interact differently with transitions oriented along different molecular axes.\n\nThe middle schematic reduces the system to four sites connected in a loop-like arrangement: a ground state on the left, an acceptor on the right, and two intermediate donor states above and below. The lower and upper pathways are driven by time-dependent field components, labeled with different dipole couplings, and each donor is coupled electronically to the acceptor. This suggests two parallel excitation routes whose relative phase or polarization could control transfer to the acceptor.\n\nOn the right are the actual molecular components: a zinc porphyrin donor and a perylene diimide acceptor. Together, the panels convey the idea that molecular donor–acceptor transfer can be steered by the handedness of incident circularly polarized light through polarization-selective excitation pathways.",
